=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** router6's iptables FORWARD chain — three DROP rules injected on the `eth2` interface (the `r6_r3` link connecting router6 to router3).

**Mechanism:** The rules `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`, `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`, and `-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP` silently discard every forwarded packet that arrives inbound on eth2. A narrow ACCEPT exception precedes them (TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 → 172.18.51.1) but is otherwise irrelevant. Traffic from the mswitch2 domain (pc15–18, laptop4, laptop5) can still leave router6 outbound on eth2 toward router3, but all return traffic from that side — pc11–14, and anything routed through router3 — is silently dropped upon re-entering eth2. The result is a one-way asymmetric blackhole.

**Localization:** router6, FORWARD chain, interface eth2. At the time of diagnosis, 20 ICMP packets (1,680 bytes — exactly 20 standard 84-byte ping frames) were already counted as dropped by the all-protocol DROP rule, corresponding to the echo-replies from pc13 (192.168.200.13) that came back via router3 during the direct-IP ping test from pc15.

**Root cause:** Injected iptables FORWARD DROP rules on router6's eth2 create an asymmetric path failure that blocks all return traffic from the router3 domain to the mswitch2 domain.

**Fix:** Remove the three DROP rules on router6:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```
Note: the user's reported laptop2 ↔ laptop3 failure was not confirmed — both hosts are on the same 172.16.10.0/24 subnet via switch1/mswitch1, a path that does not traverse router6, and bidirectional ICMP testing showed 0% loss in both directions.
=== END ===